    Bayesian-workflow skill. Ported to NumPyro + JAX with expanded
    visualization guidance. MIT licensed.
    Upstream is the bayesian-workflow skill in
    https://github.com/Learning-Bayesian-Statistics/baygent-skills, whose
    LICENSE reads "Copyright (c) 2026 Learning Bayesian Statistics"; the
    full upstream MIT text is in LICENSE-andorra. This repository imported
    the skill on 2026-06-20 (2d99584), after upstream's v1.5 (1959ef6,
    2026-06-07); which upstream version was ported is not recorded. That
    LICENSE file is unchanged since upstream's first commit (3681125).
    references/model-comparison.md additionally documents four
    arviz_stats APIs (loo_expectations, loo_metrics, loo_r2,
    bayes_factor) whose existence was pointed out by the
    `model-evaluation` skill in pymc-labs/python-analytics-skills
    (Copyright (c) 2026 Chris Fonnesbeck). That repository declares MIT
    in its README and .claude-plugin/plugin.json but ships no LICENSE
    text or copyright notice, so nothing from it is reproduced here:
    the adoption is by reference to the idea only, in the manner
    already used for llm-wiki. Every signature, prerequisite, return
    value, and caveat documented was established by executing the
    functions against arviz 1.3.0 / arviz-stats 1.3.1, not transcribed
    -- upstream's own examples do not run as written on that version.
    Prose and examples are original.
    references/jax-numerics.md additionally adapts precision- and
    stability-rule content from the `jax-equinox-numerics` and
    `jax-project-engineering` skills in mancusolab/coding-skills
    (https://github.com/mancusolab/coding-skills), MIT licensed,
    Copyright (c) 2026 Mancuso Lab; upstream license text is in
    LICENSE-coding-skills. Scope was cut to four rules plus one
    untested device rule on measured evidence — a RED baseline
    (specs/completed/red-baseline-jax-numerics-2026-08-26.md) found
    agents already apply the control-flow, PRNG, and device-count
    practices unprompted (5/5 each), so the upstream
    jit/PyTree/control-flow reference and the Equinox material were
    not ported. Prose and examples are original.
    references/model-comparison.md's "Time-series and forecast
    comparison" section is original text. It documents arviz_stats'
    lfo_cv and loo_kfold as established by running them against
    arviz-stats 1.3.3, and it cites by URL only the loo R package's
    leave-future-out vignette (Bürkner, Gabry and Vehtari; the package
    is licensed GPL (>= 3)) and the CV-FAQ. It reproduces no text or
    code from either.
    The skill cites Gelman, Vehtari, McElreath et al. (2026), "Bayesian
    Workflow" (Chapman & Hall/CRC; (c) 2020-2026 by the authors; the
    electronic edition at users.aalto.fi/~ave/Bayesian-Workflow.pdf is
    distributed "for non-commercial purposes only") by chapter and
    section number only, in original wording; it reproduces no book
    prose or figures and bundles no PDF. The book's companion repository
    (github.com/avehtari/Bayesian-Workflow, R + Stan case studies) ships
    no LICENSE file, so none of its code is ported, translated, or
    paraphrased -- case studies are cited as worked examples by chapter
    only. The earlier arXiv preprint (Gelman et al. 2020,
    arXiv:2011.01808) stays listed in references/publications.md as the
    open-access fallback. Betancourt 2020 (CC BY-NC 4.0) and Gabry et al.
    2019 (JRSS-A / arXiv:1709.01449) are likewise cited by author-year
    only; the skill reproduces no text and bundles no copies of any of
    them.
    references/gaussian-processes.md cites Solin and Särkkä (2020) and
    Riutort-Mayol et al. (2023) for the Hilbert-space approximation, and
    Bürkner, Gabry and Vehtari (2021) for leave-one-out with a joint
    Gaussian likelihood, by author-year only and in original wording; its
    examples are original and were run against the snippet gate's pinned
    NumPyro.

